<h2>存 Introduction</h2>
<p>
	The Fanya Hanwen Corpus contains texts from each Chinese dynasty, the Korean peninsula, Japan, Vietnam, and the Ryukyu Kingdom, with intent to cover Singapore and other regions in the future. Literary Chinese was and is a living language with a vibrant diversity in texts; it once enjoyed a similar status to European Latin during its heyday and should be treated as such. 
</p>

<p>
	The aim of this website, while originally built as a publicly available version of the Siku Quanshu Corpus, has turned into a project to make Literary Chinese as readable and accessible as possible. In historical traditions, Literary Chinese was read with useful annotations: Not just kanbun and hanmun annotations and so on, but also markers for names, placenames, and so on, and more. These sorts of annotations are hard to come by on the modern internet but commonplace in, say, Qing dynasty texts. Additionally, viewing options such as vertical orientation from right-to-left, removing punctuation / annotation marks, Simplified Chinese, and so on, are also hard to come by. There are many components that are simply not easy to view and rarely all in one place. 
</p>

<p>
	Examples of these aims in practice include the addition of Yuen Ren Chao's 趙元任 Old National Pronunciation 老國音, a form of Mandarin used in the Republic of China from around 1913 to 1933. This "conlect" takes elements from Wu and Middle Chinese, amongst others, to form a more inclusive Mandarin that sounds more like the older Qieyun tongue. By adding this pronunciation, the hope is to encourage readers of Literary Chinese to consider more options in their recitals! Pan-Asian pronunciations from the common Japanese and Korean to Vietnamese, Zhuang, and Tang pronunciation are also included, with hopes for more as time goes on. 
</p>

<h2>語庫讀器 <a href="/corpus_viewer/">Corpus Viewer</a></h2>
<p>
	This website features a <a href="/corpus_viewer/">Corpus Viewer</a> for navigating the massive amount of Literary Chinese available. The presentation is currently extremely unfinished - especially in navigation - but the viewer itself has many features, including but not limited to; Ruby text for pronunciation (on-right click or read all), dictionary tooltips, easy dictionary access, three themes, vertical text, horizontal text, font size and style choices, and pronunciation options.
</p>

<p>
	The aim for this Corpus Viewer is to add features to digitize traditional ways of reading Literary Chinese whilst marrying it with newer concepts. Therefore, future plans include Kanbun/Hanmun/Hánvăn annotations (with Kanbun having colour blind accessibility due to traditional red/green usage), name/placename/etc annotations typical of Qing text, instant links between citations given by writers, and more. As more data is collected, the latter will become particularly apparent.
</p>

<h2>字典 Dictionary</h2>
<p>
	This website contains a fairly robust dictionary that aims to be as inclusive as possible. At present, it contains Unihan data, Ruby text settings, ample phoneticization conventions, and the option to switch between Traditional and Simplified Chinese (as well as the original text in case of mix/var forms) whilst viewing things. Additionally, this database contains some variant form research derived from independent research, which hopes to map variants as exhaustively as possible in hopes of an eventual text normaliser. Please give feedback on this!
</p>

<p>
	Current dictionary definitions come from CC-CEDICT, Unihan, and Kangxi Dictionary. Kangxi Dictionary is regex'd to have linebreaks according to specific wording patterns in hopes of making it more readable, and so far it seems to have been successful. There are hopes to grab Shuowen Jiezi once a proper Oracle Bone Script font has been procured, as well as other historical dictionaries, periodic Wiktionary grabs, and other Public Domain sources, among others. These will also be used to annotate citations in these dictionaries. 
</p>


<h2>Text Submission</h2>
<div data-controller="corpus-submission-ticket"
     data-corpus-submission-ticket-source-value="homepage"
     data-corpus-submission-ticket-parent-path-value=""
     data-corpus-submission-ticket-lock-parent-path-value="false">
  <p>Want to add a new text to the corpus? This creates a review ticket rather than writing straight into the corpus.</p>
  <p><button type="button" class="corpus-btn corpus-btn-primary" data-action="corpus-submission-ticket#toggle">Submit a text</button></p>

  <div class="cv-text-edit-panel cv-submission-panel hidden" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="panel">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Submit a new text (creates a ticket)</h3>
    <p class="cv-hint">Use a parent path like <code>越南漢文/clean</code> or <code>越南漢文/clean/皇黎一統志</code>. The new text will still be placed inside a fresh work folder.</p>

    <form data-action="submit->corpus-submission-ticket#submit">
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Parent clean directory</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input cv-mono" placeholder="e.g. 越南漢文/clean" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="parentPath" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Work folder</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="workFolder" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Ticket title</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" value="Corpus text submission" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="title" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Short summary</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="summary" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Text type</label>
        <select class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="textType">
          <option value="source">Source text</option>
          <option value="kanbun">Kanbun</option>
          <option value="hanmun">Hanmun</option>
          <option value="hanvan">Hanvan</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>NATION</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="nation" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>WORK_TITLE</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="workTitle" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>AUTHOR</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="author" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>SOURCE</label>
        <input type="text" class="cv-input" placeholder="Write a reference here (ideally APA 7th)" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="sourceCitation" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>URL</label>
        <input type="url" class="cv-input" placeholder="https://..." data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="url" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Context and rights</label>
        <textarea class="cv-textarea" rows="4" placeholder="Explain where this text is from, whether it is public domain, what licence applies, and any context a reviewer should know." data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="contextDetails"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Reference files</label>
        <input type="file" class="cv-input" multiple data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="uploads" />
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label>Page setup</label>
        <select class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="pageMode" data-action="change->corpus-submission-ticket#togglePageMode">
          <option value="single">Single text file</option>
          <option value="multi">Multiple page files</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="singleFields">
        <div class="cv-form-row">
          <label>File name</label>
          <input type="text" class="cv-input" placeholder="Defaults to work_folder.txt" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="fileName" />
        </div>
        <div class="cv-form-row">
          <label>PAGE_TITLE</label>
          <input type="text" class="cv-input" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="pageTitle" />
        </div>
        <div class="cv-form-row">
          <label>Text body</label>
          <textarea class="cv-textarea cv-mono" rows="16" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="body"></textarea>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="multiFields" hidden>
        <div class="cv-form-row">
          <label>Pages</label>
          <div data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="pagesList"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cv-form-actions">
          <button type="button" class="corpus-btn" data-action="corpus-submission-ticket#addPage">Add page</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-row">
        <label><input type="checkbox" checked data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="storeOnDevice" /> Save ticket key on this device</label>
      </div>
      <div class="cv-form-actions">
        <button type="submit" class="corpus-btn corpus-btn-primary">Submit ticket</button>
        <button type="button" class="corpus-btn" data-action="corpus-submission-ticket#toggle">Close</button>
      </div>
    </form>

    <div class="cv-ticket-result">
      <div class="cv-ticket-status" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="status"></div>
      <div class="cv-ticket-kv">
        <div><strong>Ticket ID:</strong> <span data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="ticketId"></span></div>
        <div><strong>Ticket key (save this):</strong> <code data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="ticketKey"></code> <button type="button" class="corpus-btn" data-corpus-submission-ticket-target="copyKeyBtn" data-action="corpus-submission-ticket#copyKey">Copy</button></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>來想 Future Plans</h2>
<p>
	There are hopes to enable editing of this corpus to the public through an in-browser Pull Request system, which will be launched as the site backend improves. This will include edits to texts (correcting OCR/scribal errors, punctuation, etc), adding blurbs/synopses/summaries, and metadata details (authorship, time period, nation, etc). 
</p>

